    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1. Can protruding teeth fix themselves over time? Protruding teeth rarely correct on their own. As jaw development finishes, teeth stay in position. Without orthodontic treatment, teeth often shift further forward, increasing dental problems and health risks. 2. Do childhood habits really cause protruding teeth? Yes. Prolonged thumb sucking, pacifier use, tongue thrusting, and bottle feeding create constant pressure. Over time, these habits push teeth forward and affect jaw alignment. 3. Are braces the only option to fix protruding teeth? No. Treatment options include clear aligners, ceramic braces, functional appliances, dental bonding, and surgery in severe cases. Dentists choose based on severity and jaw structure. 4. Can protruding teeth cause jaw pain? Yes. Improper bite alignment strains jaw muscles. Over time, this imbalance leads to jaw pain, jaw issues, and chewing discomfort. 5. Does orthodontic treatment improve confidence? Yes. Correcting protruding front teeth improves speech, comfort, oral health, and self esteem. Many patients feel more confident after achieving proper alignment and a straighter smile."
    }
